<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyrenaica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyrenaica is the eastern coastal region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, named after the ancient Greek city of Cyrene. It lay on the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. Under rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qalawun">Qalawun</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> extended its control over Cyrenaica.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyrenaica.jpg" alt="Cyrenaica" width="316" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Esemono; derivative work by Felitsata · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Cyrenaica_(cropped).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ibrahim's charge covered what is now eastern Algeria, Tunisia and Tripolitania, and in theory the whole Maghreb west of <b>Cyrenaica</b>, since the territory granted him was never demarcated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Nevertheless, Italian imperialism remains deeply contested, marked by the internment of much of <b>Cyrenaica</b>'s population in camps and the execution of the resistance leader Omar Mukhtar in 1931, and by the massacre in Addis Ababa that followed the attempt on Viceroy Rodolfo Graziani's life in February 1937.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Ptolemies strengthened their control through military power and diplomatic alliances, and by the reign of Ptolemy II Philadelphus (r. 283–246 BCE) they also ruled <b>Cyrenaica</b>, Cyprus, southern Syria and parts of the Aegean, while adopting the forms of pharaonic kingship to legitimise their rule among the Egyptian population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…history: the destruction of the Sasanian Empire, the permanent loss of Byzantine power in Syria, Palestine, Egypt and <b>Cyrenaica</b>, and the extension of Arab authority from Libya to the frontiers of Central Asia and the borders of Sind, all within about a quarter of a century by a…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In Libya Fascist Italy waged a campaign of mass killings, concentration camps and the forced starvation of thousands, and expelled 100,000 Bedouin Cyrenaicans, half the population of <b>Cyrenaica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>A Fatimid army under the Berber general Habasa ibn Yusuf left Tripoli to attack Abbasid Egypt, taking the coastal route through <b>Cyrenaica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
